    id: 'sd-lb-q1',
    question: 'A service migrates from round robin to least connections and latency gets worse for some requests, not better. What likely went wrong?',
    shortAnswer: 'Least connections tracks connection count, not actual load — with persistent/multiplexed connections (HTTP/2, WebSockets, keep-alive pools) a backend can have few connections but be handling many concurrent requests over each one, so the LB under-counts its real load and keeps routing more traffic to it.',
    detailedAnswer: 'Least connections assumes connection count is a reasonable proxy for backend load, which holds when each connection roughly maps to one in-flight request (classic HTTP/1.1 without keep-alive). Once connections are long-lived and multiplexed — HTTP/2 streams over one TCP connection, WebSocket sessions, connection-pooled clients — one connection can carry many concurrent requests, so an instance handling a lot of work can still look "lightly loaded" by connection count. The fix is either switching to a request-level or latency-aware metric (e.g. weighted response-time balancing, or actual outstanding-request count rather than connection count) or, at L7, having the LB track in-flight requests per backend directly instead of relying on the L4-style connection count.',
