    shortAnswer: 'Scaling down forcibly strips carts off any terminated instance (their sessions have nowhere else to go), and scaling up doesn\'t help existing sticky clients since they keep routing to their original instance — so autoscaling can add capacity without actually relieving load on hot instances. The fix is externalizing cart state to a shared store (cache or database) so any instance can serve any request, removing the need for affinity entirely.',
    detailedAnswer: 'Sticky sessions create a hidden dependency between "which instance a client is pinned to" and "where that client\'s state lives," which conflicts with two things autoscaling assumes: that any instance can take any request, and that removing an instance is safe as long as capacity elsewhere is sufficient. With stickiness, a scale-down event either has to drain sessions gracefully (wait out a timeout, which delays the scale-down and may not be acceptable under time pressure) or drop them (data loss for in-flight carts). Scale-up is similarly compromised: because the LB doesn\'t rebalance already-pinned clients, adding fresh instances only helps new sessions, not the ones piling load onto older instances — undermining the whole point of autoscaling to redistribute load. The durable fix is to make application servers stateless with respect to session data by moving cart state into an external, shared store (e.g. a distributed cache or database), so the load balancer can route by any algorithm it likes and any instance can serve any request.',
