    question: 'A wide-column store like Cassandra advertises "tunable consistency." What does that actually mean, and why would a team choose it over a fixed consistency level?',
    shortAnswer: 'Tunable consistency means each individual read or write specifies how many replicas must acknowledge it (e.g. ONE, QUORUM, ALL) rather than the database enforcing one fixed level for everything. Teams use it to let different operations on the same cluster make different latency/consistency tradeoffs — e.g. a metrics write can use a fast, low-consistency level, while a financial-adjacent read uses QUORUM for stronger guarantees — without needing separate database clusters for each requirement.',
    detailedAnswer: 'This matters because it decouples the consistency decision from the database as a whole and makes it a per-query concern, closer to how an application actually varies in its needs. A write with consistency ONE returns as soon as a single replica acks it — fastest, but a subsequent read could hit a replica that hasn\'t received it yet. QUORUM requires a majority of replicas to ack, giving strong-enough guarantees for most purposes (a QUORUM write plus a QUORUM read guarantees the read sees the write, assuming replication factor and quorum math line up) while still tolerating some replica unavailability. The tradeoff is operational: engineers must understand and correctly choose consistency level per query, which is a new failure mode (silently picking too weak a level) that a single fixed-consistency relational database doesn\'t have.',
