    id: 'sd-repl-q4',
    question: 'Why does consistent hashing reduce data movement during resharding compared to a naive hash(key) % N scheme, and what does it cost you?',
    shortAnswer: 'With hash(key) % N, changing N changes almost every key\'s target shard because the modulo result shifts for nearly all keys. Consistent hashing places shards and keys on a ring so adding or removing one shard only affects the keys in its immediate neighborhood, remapping roughly 1/N of the keyspace instead of nearly all of it. The cost is a more complex routing layer and the need for virtual nodes to avoid uneven load when the ring has few physical shards.',
    detailedAnswer: 'Modulo hashing ties every key\'s owner to N directly, so any change to N is effectively a full-cluster data migration — infeasible to do live at scale. Consistent hashing decouples that: shards occupy fixed positions on a hash ring (often many virtual positions each, to smooth out load), and each key belongs to the next shard clockwise from its hash. Inserting a new shard only steals the keys between its ring position and the next one, and removing a shard only redistributes its keys to its neighbor(s) — bounding the migration to roughly 1/N of the data instead of nearly all of it. The cost is indirection (a ring lookup instead of simple arithmetic) and the need for virtual nodes, since with only a handful of physical shards a bare ring can land unevenly by chance.',
