    shortAnswer: 'This is a cache stampede: the key expires and every concurrent reader misses at once, all racing to the database. Fix with request coalescing (only the first miss queries the DB, others wait on that in-flight result) and/or jittered TTLs so identical keys across replicas or related keys don\'t expire in lockstep.',
    detailedAnswer: 'The root cause is that TTL expiry is a single instant shared by every reader of that key, so the cache-aside pattern\'s "miss triggers a DB load" collapses from one load to N concurrent loads exactly when the key is hottest. Two classes of fix: (1) reduce the number of DB hits per expiry — request coalescing/single-flight locks one goroutine/thread to do the reload while others block or serve the stale value briefly; a distributed lock on the cache key works similarly across processes. (2) spread out expiry so it never happens for many requests at the exact same moment — add random jitter to the TTL (e.g. 60s +/- 10s), or use a "soft TTL" where the value is marked stale but still served while one background request refreshes it (stale-while-revalidate).',
