    shortAnswer: 'Write-behind fits better here: it acknowledges the write as soon as the cache is updated and flushes to the database asynchronously in batches, minimizing write latency and reducing database load through batching — at the cost of losing acknowledged-but-unflushed writes if the cache node crashes, which the stated requirements tolerate. Write-through would add database latency to every write for a durability guarantee this use case doesn\'t need.',
    detailedAnswer: 'The deciding factor is the durability requirement: write-through makes cache and database updates part of one synchronous operation, so nothing is ever acknowledged without being durably persisted, but every write pays both the cache and database write latency plus whatever contention exists on the database at that moment. Write-behind decouples the two: the cache write is fast and immediately acknowledged, and the database write is deferred and can be batched (e.g. flush every 500ms or every N events), which both lowers latency and reduces the number of discrete database operations under high volume. The tradeoff is a durability window — anything acknowledged but not yet flushed is lost if the cache node dies. Given the stated tolerance for losing a few seconds of recent events, write-behind is the right choice; if this were financial transaction data instead of activity events, write-through (or a durable log) would be non-negotiable.',
