    question: 'You partition a Kafka topic by user ID to keep each user\'s events in order. Six months later you need more throughput and double the partition count. What breaks?',
    shortAnswer: 'Doubling partitions changes the hash-to-partition mapping for most keys, so a given user\'s events before and after the change can land on different partitions — you lose the guarantee that all of a user\'s historical and future events are ordered relative to each other on one partition. In-flight ordering within the new partition layout is fine going forward, but you can\'t assume continuity with pre-change history.',
