    question: 'Two services need data that used to live in one table via a join — Orders needs current pricing that Inventory owns. In a database-per-service world, what are the realistic options, and what do they trade off?',
    shortAnswer: 'Options: (1) Orders calls Inventory\'s API synchronously per request — simplest, but adds latency and a runtime dependency on Inventory\'s availability. (2) Inventory publishes pricing-change events and Orders keeps a local read-optimized copy — decouples availability and latency, at the cost of eventual consistency (Orders can serve stale pricing briefly). (3) A shared read-only replica or data warehouse for reporting-style access, not live transactional reads. The right choice depends on how stale a price Orders can tolerate and whether Orders can function if Inventory is briefly down.',
    detailedAnswer: 'This is the fundamental cost of database-per-service: a query that used to be free (a join in one transaction) now has to cross a service boundary, and every option for doing that has a real tradeoff, not a free lunch. Synchronous API calls keep data fresh but couple Orders\' availability and latency to Inventory\'s — if Inventory is slow or down, Orders either blocks or needs a fallback. Event-driven replication (Inventory publishes changes, Orders subscribes and maintains its own local pricing cache) decouples runtime availability entirely but introduces a staleness window, and now Orders owns a derived copy of data it does not consider itself the source of truth for, which needs its own consistency handling if events are missed or reordered. A shared reporting store is fine for analytics but should never be the path a live request depends on, since it reintroduces the shared-database coupling problem. Seniority in this answer shows in recognizing that "how stale can Orders tolerate" is a business question, not a technical one — that answer picks the pattern, not vice versa.',
