    detailedAnswer: 'Conway\'s Law is usually described as an observation, but the inverse maneuver treats it as a lever: if you want a target architecture of N independently-owned services, form N teams with clear ownership first, because left alone the org\'s existing communication structure (reporting lines, who talks to whom daily) will reassert itself in the software regardless of an architecture diagram. This matters concretely during a monolith-to-microservices migration — if the org keeps one team responsible for the entire domain, that team will keep coordinating tightly across whatever service lines get drawn, and the "microservices" will drift into a distributed monolith over time even if the initial split was clean, because the people maintaining it still work and communicate as one unit. Restructuring teams first is disruptive and political, which is why it is often skipped, but skipping it is a common reason technically sound service boundaries erode back into tight coupling within a year or two.',
