    id: 'sd-micro-q10',
    question: 'A mobile app and a web app both consume the same set of 10 backend microservices, and both clients complain about slow load times from making many round trips. How does a BFF address this, and what would you be careful about when introducing one?',
    shortAnswer: 'A BFF aggregates the fan-out — it calls the 10 services server-side (often in parallel, closer to the services on the network) and returns one shaped response per client, cutting client-side round trips and letting each client get a payload tailored to its needs (mobile trimmed, web fuller). The care point is scope creep: a BFF should stay a thin aggregation/shaping layer owned by the frontend team, not accumulate business logic that belongs in the domain services, or it becomes another monolith in disguise.',
    detailedAnswer: 'The underlying problem is that microservice decomposition, done well for the backend\'s sake, pushes composition cost onto whoever consumes the services — a client needing data from 6 services either makes 6 round trips (slow, especially over mobile networks) or the backend needs to do that composition for them. A BFF solves this by sitting server-side, close to the services, aggregating and reshaping responses per client type, and is usually owned by the team that owns that client, which also fits the general org-alignment argument for microservices (the mobile team controls its own BFF\'s release cadence independent of the web team\'s). The risk worth calling out at a senior level: a BFF that starts as thin aggregation tends to accumulate business logic over time (since it is a convenient place to put it) and can become a second monolith, or a single point where every client\'s failure gets tied to one aggregation layer\'s availability — so it needs its own resilience thinking, and its logic should stay strictly presentation/aggregation, not domain rules.',
    keyPoints: [
      'BFF cuts client-side round trips by aggregating multiple service calls server-side',
      'Lets each client type get a differently shaped response without forcing one generic API to compromise',
      'Risk: logic creep turning the BFF into a second monolith',
      'Usually owned by the frontend team it serves, consistent with the org-autonomy argument for microservices',
    ],
    seniorFollowUps: [
      'If the BFF itself becomes a single point of failure for all clients, how would you think about its own availability and resilience?',
      'Where is the line between aggregation logic that belongs in a BFF versus domain logic that should stay in the owning service?',
    ],
    topic: 'system-design',
    subtopic: 'sd-microservices',
    difficulty: 'Advanced',
