    detailedAnswer: 'The decision should be explicit and stated as a tradeoff, not assumed. Failing open is usually correct for a rate limiter because its failure mode (occasional over-admission) is far less damaging than the failure mode of failing closed (total denial of service during a dependency outage) — this mirrors the general resilience-pattern guidance of degrading gracefully rather than cascading a failure. A common middle ground is a local, coarser fallback limiter (e.g. an in-memory approximate limit per server) that engages only while the shared store is unreachable, bounding the blast radius without a full open or full closed choice.',
