    question: 'Why can\'t you just use each shard\'s local auto-increment column as a globally unique ID once a table is sharded, and why is UUIDv4 often also the wrong fix?',
    shortAnswer: 'Independent auto-increment sequences on different shards will generate the same integer on each shard, so they collide once merged; forcing a single global sequence recreates the exact write bottleneck sharding was meant to eliminate. UUIDv4 solves uniqueness with zero coordination but is random, not time-ordered, which hurts B-tree/index locality on insert and makes chronological sorting impossible without a separate timestamp field.',
    detailedAnswer: 'Snowflake-style IDs resolve both problems by packing a timestamp, a worker/machine ID, and a per-millisecond sequence number into one integer, generated locally per node with no per-ID coordination — only a one-time worker-ID assignment at startup (via something like a Zookeeper/etcd lease). Because the timestamp occupies the leading bits, IDs come out roughly sorted by creation time, which preserves index locality and lets the ID double as a natural pagination/sort key, something UUIDv4 cannot offer.',
