    shortAnswer: "Set the SLO from what actually matters to users (e.g. 99.9% of checkout requests succeed under 2s over a rolling 30-day window), derive an error budget from it (0.1% of requests), then alert on burn rate rather than the SLO itself: a fast burn rate that would exhaust the whole 30-day budget in a few hours pages immediately, while a slow burn that would take weeks to exhaust it becomes a next-business-day ticket. Use multi-window checks (e.g. both a 5-minute and 1-hour burn rate crossing a threshold) so a brief blip doesn't page for something that self-resolves.",
    detailedAnswer: 'The design has three layers. First, pick the SLI that reflects actual user experience — for checkout that\'s probably "% of checkout attempts that complete successfully within N seconds", not a proxy like CPU or a single dependency\'s health. Second, set the SLO as a target over a meaningful rolling window (30 days is common) strict enough to leave headroom under the external SLA, and derive the error budget as the allowed failure fraction that target implies. Third, and this is the part that actually drives on-call: never alert directly on "the SLO was violated" because by definition the damage is already done by the time that\'s measurable over 30 days. Instead alert on burn rate — the speed at which the budget is being consumed. A burn rate that would exhaust a 30-day budget in, say, 2 hours is an emergency and pages immediately; a burn rate that would take 2 weeks to exhaust it can wait for a ticket. Google\'s SRE approach layers multiple time windows (e.g. requiring both a short window like 5 minutes and a longer window like 1 hour to both show elevated burn) specifically to avoid paging on a transient spike that resolves on its own, while still catching sustained fast burns quickly. The practical effect: this turns "should we roll back / should we halt a risky deploy" into a budget-remaining number instead of a judgment call, and it keeps low-signal noise off the pager.',
