    id: 'sd-obs-q3',
    question: 'Your platform handles 500K requests/sec and you want distributed tracing, but tracing every request is too expensive to store. Compare head-based and tail-based sampling for this, and explain why tail-based is harder to operate.',
    shortAnswer: "Head-based sampling decides at request start (e.g. trace 1% of requests) — cheap and simple, but a rare slow or erroring request is statistically likely to be one of the 99% that got skipped, so you can lose exactly the traces you need most. Tail-based sampling buffers every span for a request and decides after it completes, so it can guarantee keeping all errors and slow outliers — but that requires routing every span belonging to the same trace to the same collector instance and holding them in memory until the request finishes, which adds infrastructure complexity, memory pressure, and latency to the collection pipeline that head-based sampling doesn't have.",
    detailedAnswer: "Head-based sampling makes the keep/drop decision at the very first hop (often via a random draw or a rate limiter) and stamps that decision into the propagated trace context so every downstream service honors it consistently — this is operationally simple and cheap since services doing the actual sampling work is close to zero, and no service needs to buffer anything. Its weakness is exactly where tracing matters most: if you sample 1%, the interesting slow or failing 0.1% of requests are randomly, not preferentially, kept, so the traces you actually want to look at during an incident are often missing. Tail-based sampling flips this by deferring the decision until the full trace is assembled, so a rule like 'keep all traces with an error or p99+ latency' can guarantee those are retained regardless of sampling rate. The operational cost is that all spans for one trace, potentially from many different services, have to arrive at the same collector process (or a coordinated tier) before a decision is made, which usually means either sticky routing on trace ID or a stateful aggregation layer, plus holding spans in memory for the duration of the longest request you care about (which could be many seconds for a slow one) before they can be dropped or exported. This is meaningfully more infrastructure than head-based sampling's stateless, per-request coin flip.",
