    id: 'sd-obs-q4',
    question: 'A request flows through an API gateway, then gets published to a Kafka topic, and a consumer service processes it asynchronously. Why does the distributed trace often break at the Kafka boundary, and how do you fix it?',
    shortAnswer: "Trace context is normally carried in thread-local state and forwarded automatically by instrumented HTTP/RPC clients, but a message queue has no such automatic carrier — the producer has to explicitly write the trace ID (and parent span ID) into the message headers, and the consumer has to explicitly read them back to start a new span with the correct parent. If that's not wired up, the consumer's spans start a brand-new trace with no link to the producer's trace, so the trace visually 'ends' at publish time.",
    detailedAnswer: "HTTP and gRPC clients that are auto-instrumented by a tracing agent transparently inject the current span's context into outgoing headers, and the corresponding server-side instrumentation reads it back — this happens without application code changes. A message broker breaks that chain because there's no synchronous request/response to hook into: the producer's send() and the consumer's poll()/onMessage() are disconnected in time and often in process. Fixing it requires the producer to explicitly serialize the current trace context into the message's header map (most tracing SDKs provide a helper for this) and the consumer to read those headers before processing and use them to start its span as a child of the producer's span rather than a fresh root. Many messaging client instrumentations (e.g. for Kafka) do this automatically once the tracing agent's Kafka instrumentation module is enabled, but it's a common gap when teams roll their own producer/consumer wrappers, use batching that merges multiple trace contexts into one poll, or fan out one message to multiple parallel consumers where the 1:1 parent-child assumption doesn't hold cleanly.",
