    id: 'sd-obs-q5',
    question: 'A team adds a `user_id` label to their HTTP request latency metric so they can debug per-customer issues, and a week later their metrics backend is falling over. What happened, and what should they have done instead?',
    shortAnswer: 'Each unique combination of label values in a metric creates a separate time series, so a label like user_id with millions of distinct values turned one metric into millions of time series, exploding memory and storage in the metrics backend — this is cardinality explosion. The fix is to keep high-cardinality identifiers out of metric labels entirely and put them in logs or trace tags instead, where each event is independent rather than pre-aggregated into a series; if per-customer debugging is genuinely needed, use traces/logs filtered by user_id, or a much lower-cardinality bucket (customer tier, region) as the metric label.',
    detailedAnswer: "Metrics systems like Prometheus pre-aggregate data into time series identified by the metric name plus its full set of label values — every unique label combination is a distinct series that has to be tracked, stored, and indexed continuously, not just at query time. A label with unbounded or very high cardinality (user IDs, request IDs, raw unnormalized URL paths, IP addresses) multiplies the number of series by the number of distinct values that label takes on, which for something like user_id in a consumer-facing service can be millions, turning one metric definition into millions of actively-tracked series. This blows up memory (each series has ongoing state), storage, and query latency, and is one of the most common ways teams accidentally take down their own monitoring stack. The correct home for high-cardinality data is logs or trace span tags, both of which are per-event rather than pre-aggregated, so cardinality there doesn't have the same multiplicative cost — you can log user_id freely and then query 'give me all logs for user_id=X' without ever having built a time series per user. If per-customer trend visibility in a dashboard is truly needed, the usual compromise is a much lower-cardinality label (customer tier, plan type, region) rather than the raw identifier.",
