    id: 'sd-obs-q6',
    question: "Your API's p99 latency is elevated. Would you reach for RED or USE metrics first, and how do the two methods complement each other in the investigation?",
    shortAnswer: "Start with RED (Rate, Errors, Duration) on the affected endpoint since it's request-centric and confirms/quantifies the symptom — is it all traffic or a subset, is it correlated with an error rate change, did request rate spike. Once RED tells you *which* endpoint and roughly *when*, switch to USE (Utilization, Saturation, Errors) on the resources that endpoint depends on — DB connection pool, thread pool, downstream service — since USE is resource-centric and tells you *why*: a saturated connection pool or a CPU-saturated instance is the actual mechanism behind the RED symptom.",
    detailedAnswer: "RED and USE are complementary lenses, not competing choices, and the investigation typically flows from one to the other. RED metrics are collected per request-handling component (an endpoint, a service) and directly reflect user-facing symptoms: Rate tells you if traffic itself shifted (a spike could just mean more load, not a regression), Errors tells you if failures correlate with the latency change (ruling out vs confirming a shared root cause), and Duration as a distribution confirms the shape of the problem (is it the whole distribution shifting, or just the tail). This narrows down *which* endpoint and *when*, but RED metrics don't explain mechanism — a service's own request/error/duration numbers don't say why duration went up. That's where USE comes in: applied to the resources the slow endpoint depends on (its DB connection pool, a downstream service call, CPU/memory on the host, a thread pool), Utilization shows how busy the resource is, Saturation shows how much work is queued waiting for it (often the earliest and most actionable signal — a resource can be at 70% utilization but already have a growing backlog), and Errors on the resource itself (e.g. connection timeouts) can point straight at the mechanism. In practice: RED localizes the symptom to a service/endpoint, USE on that service's dependencies localizes the cause to a specific resource.",
