    shortAnswer: "OFFSET forces the database to walk (or index-scan) past every skipped row before it can return the requested page, so cost grows linearly with page depth — page 2,000 means discarding ~40,000 rows first. Switching to cursor/keyset pagination (filtering on the last row's key instead of counting offsets) makes every page cost the same regardless of depth, but gives up the ability to jump directly to an arbitrary page number — users can only page forward/backward from a known position.",
    detailedAnswer: 'With OFFSET n LIMIT m, the database still has to produce and discard the first n rows in sort order before it can return rows n+1..n+m — even with an index on the sort column, that\'s an index walk of n entries, not a constant-time skip. As n grows with page depth, so does the cost, which is why shallow pages feel instant and deep pages get progressively worse — a scale issue invisible in dev/test datasets that only have a few hundred rows. Cursor (keyset) pagination replaces "skip n rows" with "give me rows after this specific key value" (WHERE id > :last_seen ORDER BY id LIMIT m), which an index can satisfy with a direct seek regardless of how deep into the dataset that key is — constant cost per page. The tradeoff is UX: keyset pagination is inherently sequential (next/previous relative to a cursor), so a "jump to page 47" control isn\'t possible without falling back to offset for that one case, and the sort key used for the cursor must be unique and stable or pages can skip or duplicate rows.',
