    shortAnswer: 'An order saga reserves inventory in step 1; before payment fails in step 2 and step 1 compensates, a completely unrelated request reads that inventory as unavailable and routes around it — a dirty read of state that turns out to be temporary. "Eventually consistent" undersells this because the intermediate state isn\'t just stale, it\'s actively actionable by other transactions. Mitigations: semantic locks (flag the record as pending and have readers special-case it), commutative updates (reserve/release as deltas rather than absolute writes so concurrent operations compose safely), or accepting the anomaly as a rare, tolerable cost given the business impact.',
