    shortAnswer: "The DB write and the Kafka publish are two independent operations with no shared transaction — if the process crashes, or Kafka is unreachable, after the DB commit but before the publish succeeds, the event is silently lost with no record it should have existed. The outbox pattern writes the business row and an outbox row for the event in the same local DB transaction, so the event's existence is exactly as durable as the order itself; a separate relay (poller or CDC) then drains the outbox and publishes at-least-once, turning an unsolvable dual-write problem into a solvable single-write-plus-relay problem.",
    detailedAnswer: 'The failure is easy to miss in a demo and painful in production because it only shows up under real crash timing — restart the process mid-request, or have the broker blip, and you get an order with no downstream event ever firing, silently. The outbox pattern\'s core insight is that you already have one thing that\'s genuinely transactional: the local database. So instead of trying to make two systems (DB, broker) commit atomically, which is the 2PC problem all over again, you make the second write also local — an outbox table row inserted in the same transaction as the business write — and defer the actual cross-system hop to a relay process that only has to solve an easier problem: reliably drain a table to a broker, at-least-once, with retries. That relay can be a simple poller (query WHERE published = false, publish, mark published) or a CDC tool like Debezium tailing the database\'s write-ahead log directly, trading operational simplicity for latency versus near-real-time delivery for extra infrastructure. Either way, the guarantee is only at-least-once — downstream consumers still need idempotent handling, since the outbox solves "don\'t lose the event," not "deliver it exactly once."',
