    shortAnswer: "Not in general — most 'exactly-once' guarantees (e.g. Kafka Streams) mean exactly-once *effect within that processing framework's transactional boundary* (input offset commit + state update + output all atomic), not a literal guarantee that duplicates never reach your code. Anything outside that boundary — a side-effecting call to an external system, a non-transactional sink — can still be invoked more than once on reprocessing after a crash, so idempotency at the boundary of your business logic is still the safer default.",
    detailedAnswer: "Exactly-once processing is achieved by combining at-least-once delivery (the only thing actually achievable over an unreliable network) with a transactional mechanism that atomically ties together consuming an input, updating internal state, and producing output, so a crash-and-retry replays the whole unit rather than leaving a partial, duplicated effect. That guarantee holds cleanly for effects fully contained inside the framework's transaction (e.g. Kafka topic-to-topic with Kafka Streams state stores). The moment a stream job calls an external HTTP API, writes to a database without transactional coordination with the offset commit, or sends an email, that call sits outside the transactional boundary — a crash after the call but before the offset commit will re-run it. Idempotent handling (dedup keys, upserts keyed by a stable ID, idempotent external APIs) is what actually protects against that, regardless of what the framework advertises.",
