    id: 'sd-rl-q3',
    question: 'Compare sliding window log and sliding window counter as fixes for the fixed-window boundary problem. What do you give up with each?',
    shortAnswer: "Sliding window log stores every request's timestamp and counts exactly how many fall in the trailing window, so it's fully accurate but memory grows with request volume per key. Sliding window counter avoids storing timestamps by keeping two fixed-window counts and weighting the previous window's count by its overlap with the current sliding window, giving O(1) memory at the cost of an approximation that assumes requests are evenly distributed within the window.",
    detailedAnswer: `The log variant is the ground truth: on each request, drop timestamps older than the window, then count what remains. It's exact but for a key handling thousands of requests per window, storing and pruning a timestamp per request is real memory and CPU pressure at scale — it doesn't degrade gracefully under high per-key volume.

The counter variant keeps just two integers per key: count in the current fixed window and count in the previous one. It estimates the sliding count as (previous_window_count * overlap_fraction) + current_window_count, where overlap_fraction is how much of the sliding window still falls in the previous fixed window. This is only exact if requests within the previous window were uniformly distributed — in practice traffic is bursty, so the estimate can be off in either direction, but it's usually close enough for rate limiting purposes (which is inherently approximate — nobody expects a rate limit to be precise to the millisecond) and is O(1) memory regardless of volume.

Most production API gateways implement the counter variant specifically because the log variant's memory profile doesn't scale to high-traffic keys.`,
