    shortAnswer: "The gap is almost always an untested assumption: the runbook's 5 minutes covered the promote-replica step, but real elapsed time included DNS TTL propagation to clients, cold-start/scale-up of standby capacity, and manual verification steps nobody had timed under real load. Fix it by running full failover drills (not just the promotion step) on a schedule, and measuring wall-clock time end-to-end including client-visible cutover.",
    detailedAnswer: "Runbook RTOs are usually measured on the narrowest step — promoting a replica to primary — and skip everything around it: DNS/client cutover time (TTL caching means 'updated' DNS can take minutes to reach all clients), the standby environment needing to scale up from idle/reduced capacity to handle full production load, cache warm-up, and manual verification gates that a human has to walk through under stress for the first time. Preventing a repeat means treating the entire path — detection, decision, promotion, traffic cutover, verification — as the thing under test, and drilling it for real (not a tabletop walkthrough) on a regular cadence, ideally as part of chaos engineering game days rather than only during real incidents.",
