    id: 'sd-svc-q2',
    question: "You're designing a multi-step order fulfillment saga (reserve inventory, charge payment, schedule shipping, notify customer) with compensations needed if any step fails. Would you choose orchestration or choreography, and what's the actual tradeoff?",
    shortAnswer: "Orchestration — once you have multiple explicit compensation paths, a central saga orchestrator (e.g. Temporal/Camunda) that models the state machine and retries/compensations in one place is far easier to test, audit, and reason about than reconstructing the flow from event handlers scattered across four services. Choreography wins for simpler, more independent reactions (e.g. 'send an analytics event on order placed') where no one needs a single view of the whole flow.",
    detailedAnswer: "The real tradeoff isn't 'which is better' — it's where you want the coupling to live. Orchestration centralizes coupling in the orchestrator, which becomes a single place to see and change the whole workflow, but it's also a component every step now technically depends on, and it can become a bottleneck for changes if many teams own steps. Choreography decouples deploys (each service just reacts to events it cares about) but scatters the actual business process across N codebases — answering 'what happens end-to-end when an order is placed' requires reading every service's event handlers, and compensation logic (what undoes step 2 if step 4 fails) is hard to keep consistent without a shared source of truth. The decision usually correlates with compensation complexity: 1-2 steps with simple undo, choreography is fine; 3+ steps with branching compensation, orchestration pays for itself.",
