    shortAnswer: "A mesh gives you uniform mTLS, retries/timeouts, load balancing, and golden-signal observability across all 40 services without each team hand-rolling a client library — real wins. It won't fix a fundamentally chatty or deeply synchronous call graph, and mesh-level automatic retries on a non-idempotent endpoint can actively make things worse by creating duplicate side effects, so it has to be paired with idempotency work, not treated as a substitute for it.",
    detailedAnswer: "Mesh value is entirely at the transport/network layer: encryption in transit without app code touching TLS, consistent retry/timeout/circuit-breaking policy pushed from a control plane instead of reimplemented per service/language, and free request-level metrics and tracing for every hop. What it structurally cannot touch is the shape of the call graph — if service A synchronously calls B, C, D in sequence, the mesh doesn't shorten that chain or make it less coupled; it just makes each hop's plumbing more consistent. Worse, naive mesh-level retries assume idempotency that may not exist at the application layer — retrying a POST /charge three times because of a transient network blip can triple-charge a customer if the handler isn't idempotent. So the rollout has to be scoped: mesh solves cross-cutting infra concerns, and any team expecting it to solve architectural coupling or business-logic correctness will be disappointed.",
