    question: "During a rolling deploy, some clients keep sending traffic to pods that were just terminated. You're using DNS-based service discovery. What's happening, and how would registry-based discovery change it?",
    shortAnswer: "DNS answers are cached at multiple layers (OS resolver, client library, sometimes intermediate caches) respecting a TTL, so a terminated pod's IP can keep being resolved and called for as long as that TTL, independent of the pod's actual health. Registry-based discovery (Consul/Eureka/etcd) with active health checks and push or short-poll updates reflects membership changes in seconds, because it's designed for frequent churn rather than the infrequent-change assumption DNS was built for.",
    detailedAnswer: 'The mismatch is architectural: DNS is a caching-friendly, eventually-consistent lookup system meant for records that change rarely. Setting a very low TTL to compensate causes its own problems (increased DNS query load, and many resolvers/clients ignore or override low TTLs anyway). A registry-based system instead maintains live health-checked membership and can push updates or be polled frequently without the caching assumptions baked into DNS infrastructure, which is why Kubernetes internally uses more than plain DNS resolution for Service routing (kube-proxy programs actual routing rules, not just a DNS answer) even though it also exposes a DNS name for convenience.',
